// Curated concept chunks used to ground each generated question (simple RAG:
// one chunk is retrieved per request and passed to the model as context).
module.exports = [
  // --- Data structures & algorithms ---
  { id: "ds-1", category: "Data Structures", topic: "Hash Tables", content: "A hash table maps keys to values using a hash function to compute an index into an array of buckets. Average-case lookup, insert, and delete are O(1), but collisions (two keys hashing to the same bucket) can degrade this to O(n) in the worst case. Collision resolution strategies include chaining (linked lists per bucket) and open addressing (probing for the next free slot)." },
  { id: "ds-2", category: "Data Structures", topic: "Binary Search Trees", content: "A binary search tree keeps every left subtree less than its node and every right subtree greater, giving O(log n) search, insert, and delete when balanced. An unbalanced tree (e.g. built from sorted input) degrades to a linked list with O(n) operations, which is why self-balancing variants like AVL or red-black trees exist." },
  { id: "ds-3", category: "Algorithms", topic: "Big-O Complexity", content: "Big-O notation describes how an algorithm's runtime or memory use grows as input size increases, focusing on the dominant term and ignoring constants. O(1) is constant, O(log n) grows slowly (e.g. binary search), O(n) is linear, O(n log n) is typical of good sorting algorithms, and O(n^2) or worse indicates nested iteration over the input." },
  { id: "ds-4", category: "Algorithms", topic: "Dynamic Programming", content: "Dynamic programming solves problems by breaking them into overlapping subproblems and storing (memoizing) their results to avoid recomputation. It applies when a problem has optimal substructure (an optimal solution can be built from optimal solutions to subproblems) and overlapping subproblems, as in Fibonacci, knapsack, or edit distance." },
  { id: "ds-5", category: "Algorithms", topic: "Graph Traversal", content: "Breadth-first search (BFS) explores a graph level by level using a queue and finds the shortest path in an unweighted graph. Depth-first search (DFS) explores as far as possible along each branch using a stack (or recursion) before backtracking, and is well suited to cycle detection and topological sorting." },
  { id: "ds-6", category: "Algorithms", topic: "Sorting Algorithms", content: "Comparison sorts like merge sort and quicksort run in O(n log n) average time; merge sort is stable and guarantees O(n log n) worst case at the cost of O(n) extra space, while quicksort is typically faster in practice but degrades to O(n^2) on adversarial pivots. Counting sort and radix sort can beat O(n log n) but only work for restricted input types like small-range integers." },

  // --- System design ---
  { id: "sd-1", category: "System Design", topic: "Load Balancing", content: "A load balancer distributes incoming requests across multiple backend servers to improve availability and throughput. Common strategies include round robin, least connections, and consistent hashing (which minimizes redistribution when servers are added or removed, important for caching layers)." },
  { id: "sd-2", category: "System Design", topic: "Caching Strategies", content: "Caching stores frequently accessed data closer to the consumer to reduce latency and backend load. Cache-aside (lazy loading) fetches from the source on a miss and populates the cache, write-through writes to cache and source simultaneously, and write-back writes to cache first and flushes to source later, trading durability for write speed." },
  { id: "sd-3", category: "System Design", topic: "CAP Theorem", content: "The CAP theorem states that a distributed system can only guarantee two of three properties during a network partition: consistency (every read gets the latest write), availability (every request gets a response), and partition tolerance (the system keeps working despite dropped or delayed messages). Since partitions are unavoidable at scale, real systems choose between CP (favoring consistency) and AP (favoring availability)." },
  { id: "sd-4", category: "System Design", topic: "Message Queues", content: "A message queue decouples producers from consumers by buffering messages between them, allowing services to scale independently and absorb traffic spikes. Queues can offer at-least-once delivery (messages may be processed more than once, requiring idempotent consumers) or exactly-once semantics, which is harder to guarantee and usually more expensive." },
  { id: "sd-5", category: "System Design", topic: "Database Sharding", content: "Sharding splits a large dataset across multiple database instances, each holding a subset of the rows, to scale writes and storage beyond a single machine. A shard key determines how data is distributed; a poorly chosen key can create hot shards that receive disproportionate traffic, undermining the benefit of sharding." },
  { id: "sd-6", category: "System Design", topic: "Rate Limiting", content: "Rate limiting caps how many requests a client can make in a given window to protect a service from overload or abuse. The token bucket algorithm allows bursts up to a bucket capacity while refilling tokens at a steady rate, while the sliding window log or counter approach tracks requests within a rolling time window for smoother enforcement." },

  // --- Databases ---
  { id: "db-1", category: "Databases", topic: "ACID Transactions", content: "ACID describes guarantees a database transaction provides: atomicity (all operations succeed or none do), consistency (the database moves between valid states), isolation (concurrent transactions don't interfere, enforced via isolation levels like read committed or serializable), and durability (committed data survives crashes)." },
  { id: "db-2", category: "Databases", topic: "Database Indexing", content: "An index is a separate data structure, typically a B-tree, that lets the database find rows matching a query without scanning the whole table. Indexes speed up reads but slow down writes (since the index must be updated too) and consume extra storage, so they are usually added on columns used frequently in WHERE clauses or joins." },
  { id: "db-3", category: "Databases", topic: "Normalization", content: "Database normalization organizes relational tables to reduce data redundancy and avoid update anomalies, typically by progressing through normal forms (1NF removes repeating groups, 2NF removes partial dependencies, 3NF removes transitive dependencies). Denormalization intentionally reintroduces redundancy to optimize read performance at the cost of update complexity." },
  { id: "db-4", category: "Databases", topic: "SQL vs NoSQL", content: "Relational (SQL) databases enforce a fixed schema and strong consistency, and excel at complex joins and transactions. NoSQL databases (document, key-value, column-family, graph) relax schema constraints and often trade strict consistency for horizontal scalability and flexible data models, fitting workloads with high write volume or evolving schemas." },

  // --- OOP & design patterns ---
  { id: "oop-1", category: "OOP", topic: "SOLID Principles", content: "SOLID is a set of five object-oriented design principles: Single responsibility (a class should have one reason to change), Open/closed (open for extension, closed for modification), Liskov substitution (subtypes must be usable wherever their base type is expected), Interface segregation (prefer many specific interfaces over one general one), and Dependency inversion (depend on abstractions, not concrete implementations)." },
  { id: "oop-2", category: "Design Patterns", topic: "Singleton Pattern", content: "The singleton pattern restricts a class to a single instance and provides a global access point to it, often used for shared resources like configuration or connection pools. It is frequently criticized for introducing hidden global state and making unit testing harder, since it's difficult to substitute a mock instance." },
  { id: "oop-3", category: "Design Patterns", topic: "Observer Pattern", content: "The observer pattern defines a one-to-many dependency where a subject notifies a list of registered observers automatically when its state changes, without the subject needing to know their concrete types. It underlies event systems, pub/sub messaging, and reactive UI frameworks." },
  { id: "oop-4", category: "OOP", topic: "Composition vs Inheritance", content: "Inheritance creates an 'is-a' relationship where a subclass reuses and extends a parent class's behavior, but can lead to fragile hierarchies as depth increases. Composition creates a 'has-a' relationship by building objects out of other objects' behavior, favored by the principle 'favor composition over inheritance' because it keeps components independently testable and swappable." },

  // --- Concurrency & networking ---
  { id: "cc-1", category: "Concurrency", topic: "Race Conditions", content: "A race condition occurs when multiple threads or processes access shared state concurrently and the outcome depends on the unpredictable timing of their execution. It is typically prevented with synchronization primitives like locks, mutexes, or atomic operations, though excessive locking can introduce deadlocks or reduce throughput." },
  { id: "cc-2", category: "Concurrency", topic: "Deadlocks", content: "A deadlock happens when two or more threads each hold a resource the other needs and neither can proceed, permanently blocking. The four necessary conditions (mutual exclusion, hold and wait, no preemption, circular wait) are the basis for deadlock prevention strategies, such as always acquiring locks in a fixed global order." },
  { id: "net-1", category: "Networking", topic: "TCP vs UDP", content: "TCP is connection-oriented and guarantees ordered, reliable delivery through acknowledgments and retransmission, making it suitable for web traffic and file transfer but adding latency overhead. UDP is connectionless with no delivery guarantees, offering lower latency and less overhead, which suits real-time applications like video calls or gaming where occasional packet loss is tolerable." },

  // --- Testing & practices ---
  { id: "test-1", category: "Testing", topic: "Unit vs Integration Testing", content: "Unit tests verify a single function or class in isolation, typically using mocks or stubs for dependencies, and run fast enough to execute on every code change. Integration tests verify that multiple components work together correctly (e.g. a service talking to a real database), catching interface mismatches that unit tests, with their mocked dependencies, cannot." },
  { id: "test-2", category: "Software Practices", topic: "CI/CD", content: "Continuous integration automatically builds and tests code on every commit to catch integration problems early, while continuous delivery/deployment automates releasing that code to staging or production once it passes those checks. Together they reduce the risk and manual effort of shipping software by replacing large, infrequent releases with small, frequently verified ones." },

  // --- ML fundamentals ---
  { id: "ml-1", category: "ML Fundamentals", topic: "Overfitting vs Underfitting", content: "Overfitting occurs when a model learns noise and specific patterns in the training data that don't generalize, showing low training error but high validation error. Underfitting occurs when a model is too simple to capture the underlying pattern, showing high error on both training and validation data. Regularization, more data, or simpler models address overfitting; more capacity or features address underfitting." },
  { id: "ml-2", category: "ML Fundamentals", topic: "Bias-Variance Tradeoff", content: "Bias is error from overly simplistic assumptions in a model (underfitting), while variance is error from sensitivity to small fluctuations in the training set (overfitting). Total expected error decomposes into bias squared, variance, and irreducible noise, so reducing one often increases the other, which is why model complexity must be tuned rather than maximized." },
  { id: "ml-3", category: "ML Fundamentals", topic: "Regularization (L1/L2)", content: "Regularization adds a penalty term to the loss function to discourage overly complex models and reduce overfitting. L1 (Lasso) penalizes the sum of absolute weight values and tends to push some weights exactly to zero, performing implicit feature selection. L2 (Ridge) penalizes the sum of squared weights, shrinking them smoothly toward zero without eliminating them entirely." },
  { id: "ml-4", category: "ML Fundamentals", topic: "Cross-Validation", content: "K-fold cross-validation splits the training data into k subsets, trains on k-1 of them and validates on the remaining one, repeating k times so every subset is used for validation once. Averaging the resulting scores gives a more reliable estimate of a model's generalization performance than a single train/validation split, at the cost of k times the training compute." },
  { id: "ml-5", category: "Model Evaluation", topic: "Precision vs Recall", content: "Precision is the fraction of predicted positives that are actually positive (TP / (TP + FP)), while recall is the fraction of actual positives the model correctly identifies (TP / (TP + FN)). There is usually a tradeoff between them controlled by the classification threshold; the F1 score, their harmonic mean, balances both when neither alone is a sufficient metric." },
  { id: "ml-6", category: "Model Evaluation", topic: "Confusion Matrix", content: "A confusion matrix tabulates a classifier's predictions against actual labels, breaking them into true positives, true negatives, false positives, and false negatives. It is the basis for deriving accuracy, precision, recall, and specificity, and is especially useful for spotting class imbalance problems that accuracy alone would hide." },
  { id: "ml-7", category: "Optimization", topic: "Gradient Descent", content: "Gradient descent iteratively updates model parameters in the direction that most reduces the loss function, scaled by a learning rate. Too high a learning rate can overshoot minima and diverge; too low makes convergence slow. Variants like mini-batch and stochastic gradient descent trade off gradient accuracy against computation per update, and momentum-based optimizers like Adam adapt the effective learning rate per parameter." },
  { id: "ml-8", category: "Optimization", topic: "Learning Rate Scheduling", content: "A learning rate schedule adjusts the learning rate during training, commonly starting higher for fast initial progress and decaying it (step decay, cosine annealing, or warm-up followed by decay) as training progresses to allow finer convergence near a minimum. Poor scheduling can cause a model to plateau early or oscillate without converging." },

  // --- Neural networks & deep learning ---
  { id: "nn-1", category: "Neural Networks", topic: "Backpropagation", content: "Backpropagation computes the gradient of the loss with respect to every weight in a neural network by applying the chain rule backward from the output layer to the input layer, reusing intermediate results for efficiency. This gradient is then used by an optimizer like gradient descent to update the weights." },
  { id: "nn-2", category: "Neural Networks", topic: "Activation Functions", content: "Activation functions introduce non-linearity so a neural network can model complex relationships rather than collapsing into a single linear transformation. ReLU (max(0, x)) is cheap to compute and avoids vanishing gradients for positive inputs but can 'die' for negative inputs; sigmoid and tanh saturate at extremes, causing vanishing gradients in deep networks." },
  { id: "nn-3", category: "Neural Networks", topic: "Vanishing/Exploding Gradients", content: "In deep networks, gradients can shrink toward zero (vanishing) or grow uncontrollably (exploding) as they're propagated backward through many layers, especially with saturating activations or poorly scaled weights. Techniques like careful weight initialization, batch normalization, residual connections, and gradient clipping mitigate these issues." },
  { id: "nn-4", category: "Deep Learning", topic: "Convolutional Neural Networks", content: "CNNs use learnable filters that slide across an input (typically an image) to detect local patterns like edges or textures, with parameter sharing across spatial positions making them far more efficient than fully connected layers for grid-like data. Pooling layers then downsample feature maps to reduce dimensionality and add translation invariance." },
  { id: "nn-5", category: "Deep Learning", topic: "Transformer Attention", content: "Self-attention lets a model weigh the relevance of every other token in a sequence when encoding a given token, computed via query, key, and value projections, rather than processing tokens strictly in order as RNNs do. This parallelizability and ability to capture long-range dependencies is why transformers replaced recurrent architectures in most modern NLP and increasingly vision tasks." },
  { id: "nn-6", category: "Deep Learning", topic: "Dropout", content: "Dropout randomly zeroes a fraction of neuron activations during each training step, forcing the network to not rely too heavily on any single neuron and reducing co-adaptation between them. This acts as a regularizer that reduces overfitting; dropout is disabled at inference time, with activations typically rescaled to account for its absence." },

  // --- MLOps & applied ML ---
  { id: "mlops-1", category: "MLOps", topic: "Train/Validation/Test Split", content: "The training set is used to fit model parameters, the validation set is used to tune hyperparameters and make model-selection decisions without touching the test set, and the test set gives a final, unbiased estimate of generalization performance, evaluated only once model development is finished." },
  { id: "mlops-2", category: "MLOps", topic: "Data Leakage", content: "Data leakage occurs when information that would not be available at prediction time (such as future data, or features derived using the target) leaks into training, causing inflated validation performance that fails to hold in production. Common sources include preprocessing (e.g. scaling) fit on the full dataset before splitting, or duplicate records spanning train and test sets." },
  { id: "mlops-3", category: "MLOps", topic: "Model Drift", content: "Model drift is the degradation of a deployed model's performance over time as the statistical properties of incoming data diverge from the training distribution (data drift) or the relationship between features and target changes (concept drift). Monitoring input distributions and prediction quality in production, with scheduled retraining, is the standard mitigation." },
  { id: "mlops-4", category: "Applied ML", topic: "Feature Engineering", content: "Feature engineering transforms raw data into representations that make patterns easier for a model to learn, such as encoding categorical variables, scaling numeric features, or creating interaction terms. Well-chosen features often improve performance more than switching model architectures, especially for classical ML models that don't learn representations automatically the way deep networks do." },

  // --- Capstone-aligned: speech ASR, NLP extraction, fine-tuning, deployment ---
  { id: "cap-1", category: "Speech ML", topic: "Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR)", content: "ASR converts spoken audio into text, typically by combining an acoustic model (maps audio features to phonemes or sub-word units) with a language model (scores which word sequences are plausible). End-to-end models like Whisper fold both into one network trained directly on paired audio-transcript data, which is why they handle accents and background noise more gracefully than older pipeline-based systems." },
  { id: "cap-2", category: "Speech ML", topic: "Word Error Rate (WER)", content: "Word Error Rate is the standard metric for ASR quality: it counts the substitutions, deletions, and insertions needed to turn the model's transcript into the reference transcript, divided by the number of words in the reference. A lower WER is better, but WER alone hides where errors happen, a model can have a decent WER overall while still badly mangling the specific product names or prices a downstream system depends on." },
  { id: "cap-3", category: "Speech ML", topic: "Model Hallucination in ASR", content: "Hallucination in ASR is when the model outputs fluent-sounding words that were never actually spoken, often during silence, background noise, or non-speech audio, rather than simply mis-transcribing a real word. It's especially dangerous for automated pipelines because the output looks plausible, so downstream systems have no obvious signal that the transcript is fabricated." },
  { id: "cap-4", category: "Speech ML", topic: "Noise Robustness & Data Augmentation", content: "A model trained mostly on clean audio often degrades sharply in noisy real-world conditions. Noise robustness is commonly improved by augmenting training audio with real recorded noise samples (such as the MUSAN or QUT-NOISE corpora) mixed in at varying volumes, so the model learns to recognize speech under conditions similar to deployment rather than only in a quiet studio." },
  { id: "cap-5", category: "Speech ML", topic: "Text-to-Speech (TTS) Synthesis", content: "TTS converts text into spoken audio, the reverse of ASR. In an ML pipeline it's often used to generate synthetic audio from labeled text at scale, which can then be fed through an ASR + extraction pipeline to validate the whole system end to end before any real recorded audio is available." },
  { id: "cap-6", category: "NLP", topic: "Information Extraction / Slot Filling", content: "Information extraction pulls structured data (like entities, categories, or field values) out of unstructured text. Slot filling is a common framing: given a known schema (for example product, price, outcome), the model's job is to find the span of text that fills each slot, rather than generating free-form text." },
  { id: "cap-7", category: "NLP", topic: "Named Entity Recognition", content: "Named Entity Recognition (NER) labels spans of text as belonging to predefined categories, such as product names, prices, or dates. It's a narrower, more established task than general information extraction, and is often used as one component inside a larger extraction pipeline that also has to infer relationships between the entities it finds." },
  { id: "cap-8", category: "NLP", topic: "Code-Switching & Low-Resource Languages", content: "Code-switching is when a speaker mixes two or more languages within the same conversation or even the same sentence, common in multilingual settings. Models trained mostly on monolingual, high-resource text (like English) often perform far worse on code-switched or low-resource language input, since the patterns of mixing rarely appear in standard training corpora." },
  { id: "cap-9", category: "MLOps", topic: "Fine-Tuning Pretrained Models", content: "Fine-tuning takes a model already trained on a large general dataset and continues training it on a smaller, task-specific dataset, adapting its existing knowledge rather than learning from scratch. It's far cheaper than training from scratch and usually performs better on narrow tasks, but the smaller fine-tuning dataset means it's easy to overfit if not monitored carefully." },
  { id: "cap-10", category: "MLOps", topic: "Catastrophic Forgetting", content: "Catastrophic forgetting is when fine-tuning a model on new, narrow data causes it to lose performance on the broader task it was originally good at. It's a key risk whenever a general model (like an ASR or extraction model) is specialized to one domain, since the very updates that improve domain performance can overwrite previously learned general patterns." },
  { id: "cap-11", category: "MLOps", topic: "Synthetic Data Generation", content: "Synthetic data generation uses a model (often an LLM) to produce artificial training examples when real labeled data is scarce or expensive to collect. It lets development start before any real data exists, but synthetic data can carry its own biases or unrealistic patterns, so models trained only on it are usually validated against real data before being trusted." },
  { id: "cap-12", category: "MLOps", topic: "Domain Adaptation", content: "Domain adaptation is the problem of making a model trained on one data distribution (like generic audio or text) perform well on a different, related distribution it will actually see in deployment (like noisy in-shop audio in a specific set of languages). It's related to fine-tuning but specifically focuses on the shift in input distribution, not just the task." },
  { id: "cap-13", category: "MLOps", topic: "Active Learning / Human-in-the-Loop", content: "Active learning, often implemented via human-in-the-loop correction, improves a model by having humans label or fix the cases the model is currently getting wrong, then feeding those corrections back into training. It's more data-efficient than randomly collecting more labels, since effort is spent exactly where the model is weakest." },
  { id: "cap-14", category: "Model Evaluation", topic: "Benchmarking Model Variants", content: "Benchmarking compares multiple model options (for example different ASR models) on the same metrics under the same conditions, so a choice can be made on evidence rather than assumption. A thorough benchmark reports performance under the conditions the model will actually face in deployment, such as noisy audio, not just clean best-case conditions, since a model that wins on clean audio can lose badly once real-world noise is introduced." },
  { id: "cap-15", category: "System Design", topic: "Data Minimization & Privacy by Design", content: "Data minimization is the principle of collecting and retaining only the data actually needed, for only as long as it's needed, rather than keeping everything indefinitely 'just in case'. A common pattern is to process sensitive raw data (like audio) into a less sensitive derived form (like a text transcript) and then delete the raw data immediately, reducing what's exposed if the system is ever breached." },
  { id: "cap-16", category: "Software Practices", topic: "MVP & Iterative Validation", content: "A minimum viable product (MVP) is the smallest version of a system that still tests the core idea, built before investing in a full, polished implementation. In an ML-heavy system, it's common to validate each pipeline stage separately (for example proving a model works on a notebook with synthetic data) before building the full application around it, since fixing a bad core model is far cheaper early than after a full app is built on top of it." },
  { id: "cap-17", category: "Applied ML", topic: "Synthetic vs Real Data Evaluation", content: "A model can perform very well on synthetic or held-out training-distribution data while performing much worse on real-world data, because synthetic data rarely captures every quirk of real conditions. This is why models developed on synthetic data are typically evaluated again on a small set of real examples before being trusted, treating the synthetic-data score as a lower bound on how much work remains, not a final answer." },
  { id: "cap-18", category: "System Design", topic: "Voice User Interfaces (VUI)", content: "A voice user interface lets people interact with a system by speaking and listening rather than typing and reading, which lowers the barrier for users with limited literacy or limited experience with screens and apps. Designing a good VUI usually means keeping prompts short, confirming important actions out loud, and giving the user an easy way to correct the system when it misunderstands them." },
  { id: "cap-19", category: "System Design", topic: "Chaining ML Pipelines", content: "A chained ML pipeline passes the output of one model as the input to the next (for example ASR output feeding into an extraction model), rather than training one model to do everything end to end. Chaining is easier to build and debug stage by stage, but errors compound: a mistake in an early stage (like a mis-transcribed word) can cause a cascading failure in every stage after it." },
  { id: "cap-20", category: "Model Evaluation", topic: "Latency vs Accuracy Tradeoffs", content: "Choosing a model for production is rarely about accuracy alone, a more accurate model that's too slow or too expensive to run at the needed scale may be the wrong choice. Model selection usually weighs accuracy against latency, cost per inference, and robustness to real-world conditions together, rather than picking whichever model tops a single leaderboard metric." },

    // --- Math: probability, statistics, linear algebra, calculus, information theory ---
  { id: "math-1", category: "Probability", topic: "Bayes' Theorem", calc: true, content: "Bayes' theorem updates a belief after seeing evidence: P(A|B) = P(B|A) x P(A) / P(B), where P(B) = P(B|A)P(A) + P(B|not A)P(not A). Worked example: 1% of people have a disease (prior 0.01). The test is positive for 90% of sick people (0.9) and falsely positive for 9% of healthy people (0.09). P(positive) = 0.9 x 0.01 + 0.09 x 0.99 = 0.0981, so P(sick | positive) = 0.009 / 0.0981 = about 0.092, or 9.2%. Lesson: when the thing you look for is rare, even a good test gives mostly false alarms. Naive Bayes spam filters use this same rule to pick the most likely class given the words in a message." },
  { id: "math-2", category: "Probability", topic: "Conditional Probability and Independence", calc: true, content: "P(A and B) = P(A) x P(B|A). Two events are independent when knowing one tells you nothing about the other, so P(A and B) = P(A) x P(B). Worked example: a bag has 3 red and 2 blue balls. Drawing two balls without putting the first back, P(red then red) = 3/5 x 2/4 = 6/20 = 0.3. If the first ball is put back, the draws are independent and P(red then red) = 3/5 x 3/5 = 0.36. The chain rule multiplies conditional probabilities step by step and is how a language model scores a sentence: P(w1, w2, w3) = P(w1) x P(w2|w1) x P(w3|w1,w2)." },
  { id: "math-3", category: "Probability", topic: "Expected Value and Variance", calc: true, content: "Expected value is the long-run average: E[X] = sum of (value x probability). Variance measures spread: Var(X) = E[X^2] - (E[X])^2, and standard deviation is its square root. Worked example: a fair six-sided die has E[X] = (1+2+3+4+5+6)/6 = 3.5, E[X^2] = 91/6 = 15.17, so Var = 15.17 - 12.25 = 2.92. Another example: a game pays 10 with probability 0.2 and 0 otherwise, so E = 10 x 0.2 = 2. Use case: the average loss over a batch of training examples is an expected value, and feature scaling uses the standard deviation." },
  { id: "math-4", category: "Statistics", topic: "Gaussian Distribution and Z-scores", calc: true, content: "The Gaussian (normal) distribution is the bell curve described by a mean (centre) and standard deviation (spread). About 68% of values fall within 1 standard deviation of the mean, 95% within 2, and 99.7% within 3. A z-score tells you how many standard deviations a value is from the mean: z = (x - mean) / sd. Worked example: exam scores have mean 70 and sd 10, so a score of 90 has z = (90 - 70) / 10 = 2, which beats about 97.7% of students. Use case: standardising features (subtract the mean, divide by the sd) so no single feature dominates training." },
  { id: "math-5", category: "Statistics", topic: "Maximum Likelihood Estimation", calc: true, content: "Maximum likelihood picks the parameter values that make the observed data most probable. For independent data the likelihood is a product of probabilities, so we maximise the log-likelihood instead: it turns the product into a sum and avoids multiplying many tiny numbers into zero. Worked example: 7 heads in 10 flips gives likelihood L(p) = p^7 x (1-p)^3, which peaks at p = 7/10 = 0.7. For a Gaussian, the best mean is the sample mean and the best variance is the average of squared deviations (divide by n). Training a classifier with cross-entropy loss is the same as maximising log-likelihood." },
  { id: "math-6", category: "Probability", topic: "Softmax and Cross-Entropy Loss", calc: true, content: "Softmax turns raw scores (logits) into probabilities that add up to 1: softmax(z_i) = e^(z_i) / sum of e^(z_j). Worked example: logits [2, 1, 0] give e^2 = 7.389, e^1 = 2.718, e^0 = 1, total 11.107, so probabilities are 0.665, 0.245, 0.090. Cross-entropy loss for one example is -ln(probability given to the true class). If class 0 is true, loss = -ln(0.665) = 0.41. A confident correct answer gives a small loss, and a confident wrong answer gives a huge loss. Use case: the last step of almost every classifier, including a model that labels a spoken sentence as inquiry, sale or negotiation." },
  { id: "math-7", category: "Linear Algebra", topic: "Dot Product and Cosine Similarity", calc: true, content: "The dot product multiplies matching numbers of two vectors and adds them up: a . b = a1 x b1 + a2 x b2 + ... Cosine similarity measures the angle between vectors, ignoring their length: cos = (a . b) / (|a| x |b|), where |a| = sqrt(a1^2 + a2^2 + ...). It is 1 for the same direction, 0 for perpendicular (unrelated) and -1 for opposite. Worked example: a = (3, 4) and b = (4, 3) give a . b = 24, |a| = 5, |b| = 5, so cos = 24/25 = 0.96. Use case: finding the document or word whose embedding points in the most similar direction." },
  { id: "math-8", category: "Linear Algebra", topic: "Matrix Shapes and Parameter Counts", calc: true, content: "Multiplying an (a x b) matrix by a (b x c) matrix needs the two inner sizes to match and gives an (a x c) result. A dense layer computes y = Wx + b: with n inputs and m neurons, W is m x n and b has m values, so parameters = m x n + m. Worked example: 4 input features and 3 neurons give a 3x4 weight matrix (12 numbers) plus 3 biases = 15 parameters. A batch of 32 examples with 4 features is a 32x4 matrix, and multiplying by a 4x3 weight matrix gives 32x3 outputs. Use case: checking that layer shapes line up before training, and estimating how big a model is." },
  { id: "math-9", category: "Calculus", topic: "Gradients, Chain Rule and a Gradient Descent Step", calc: true, content: "The gradient says which way and how steeply the loss rises, so we step the opposite way: w_new = w_old - learning_rate x gradient. Worked example: loss L(w) = (w - 3)^2 has derivative 2(w - 3). At w = 5 the gradient is 4. With learning rate 0.1, w_new = 5 - 0.1 x 4 = 4.6, moving toward the best value w = 3. The chain rule says if y = f(g(x)) then dy/dx = f'(g(x)) x g'(x), and backpropagation applies it layer by layer from the output back to the input. A learning rate that is too big overshoots, and one that is too small is slow." },
  { id: "math-10", category: "Information Theory", topic: "Entropy and KL Divergence", calc: true, content: "Entropy measures surprise or uncertainty: H = -sum of p x log2(p), in bits. A fair coin has H = 1 bit. A coin that lands heads 90% of the time has H = -(0.9 x log2 0.9 + 0.1 x log2 0.1) = 0.137 + 0.332 = about 0.47 bits, and a coin that always lands heads has 0 bits. Cross-entropy H(p,q) = -sum of p x log(q) measures how well a model q describes the real distribution p, and equals H(p) + KL(p||q). KL divergence is never negative, is 0 only when the two distributions are equal, and is not symmetric. Use case: the training loss of language models." },
  { id: "math-11", category: "Linear Algebra", topic: "PCA, Eigenvectors and Explained Variance", calc: true, content: "Principal Component Analysis (PCA) finds new axes along which the data varies the most, so you can keep fewer dimensions and lose little information. The axes are eigenvectors of the covariance matrix, and each eigenvalue is the variance along its axis. Explained variance ratio = eigenvalue / sum of all eigenvalues. Worked example: eigenvalues 6, 3 and 1 give ratios 0.6, 0.3 and 0.1, so keeping two components keeps 90% of the variance. Data should be centred (mean subtracted) first. Use case: squashing 300-number word vectors down to 2 numbers so they can be plotted." },
  { id: "math-12", category: "ML Fundamentals", topic: "Sigmoid and Logistic Regression", calc: true, content: "The sigmoid function squashes any number into the range 0 to 1: sigmoid(z) = 1 / (1 + e^(-z)). Key values: sigmoid(0) = 0.5, sigmoid(2) is about 0.88 and sigmoid(-2) is about 0.12. Logistic regression computes z = w . x + b, then probability = sigmoid(z), and predicts class 1 when the probability is at least 0.5, which means z >= 0. Worked example: w = (2, -1), b = -1 and x = (1, 1) give z = 2 - 1 - 1 = 0, so the probability is exactly 0.5. Use case: yes or no predictions such as 'will this customer pay later'." },
  { id: "math-13", category: "Model Evaluation", topic: "Precision, Recall and F1 by Hand", calc: true, content: "Worked example: a spam filter catches 40 spam emails (TP = 40), wrongly flags 10 good emails (FP = 10) and misses 20 spam emails (FN = 20). Precision = TP / (TP + FP) = 40/50 = 0.80. Recall = TP / (TP + FN) = 40/60 = 0.667. F1 = 2 x P x R / (P + R) = 2 x 0.8 x 0.667 / 1.467 = 0.727. Accuracy can mislead on imbalanced data: if 99% of emails are not spam, a model that always says 'not spam' is 99% accurate but has recall 0. Use case: choosing a threshold when missing a real case costs more than a false alarm." },

  // --- Clustering, mixture models and HMMs ---
  { id: "clu-1", category: "Clustering", topic: "K-means Algorithm", content: "K-means groups unlabelled points into k clusters. Steps: (1) pick k starting centroids, (2) assign every point to its nearest centroid, (3) move each centroid to the mean of its assigned points, (4) repeat steps 2 and 3 until assignments stop changing. It minimises the within-cluster sum of squared distances (inertia). Weaknesses: you must choose k, results depend on the starting centroids (k-means++ picks spread-out starts to help), and it prefers round, similar-sized clusters. Use case: grouping shop customers by buying behaviour when there are no labels." },
  { id: "clu-2", category: "Clustering", topic: "K-means Centroid and Distance Math", calc: true, content: "A centroid is the average position of its points: add up each coordinate and divide by the number of points. Worked example: points (1,2), (3,4) and (5,0) give centroid ((1+3+5)/3, (2+4+0)/3) = (3, 2). Assignment uses Euclidean distance d = sqrt((x1-x2)^2 + (y1-y2)^2). A point at (2,2) with centroids A = (1,1) and B = (5,5) has dA = sqrt(2) = 1.41 and dB = sqrt(18) = 4.24, so it joins cluster A. After assignment, each centroid is recomputed as the new mean of its cluster." },
  { id: "clu-3", category: "Clustering", topic: "Choosing k: Elbow and Silhouette", calc: true, content: "Elbow method: plot inertia against k and pick the k where the curve stops dropping sharply. Silhouette score for one point is s = (b - a) / max(a, b), where a is its average distance to points in its own cluster and b is its average distance to the nearest other cluster. s lies between -1 and 1: near 1 means well placed, near 0 means on a border, negative means probably in the wrong cluster. Worked example: a = 2 and b = 6 give s = (6 - 2) / 6 = 0.67. Inertia always falls as k grows (k equal to the number of points gives inertia 0), so inertia alone cannot choose k." },
  { id: "clu-4", category: "Clustering", topic: "Gaussian Mixture Models and EM", calc: true, content: "A Gaussian Mixture Model (GMM) says the data came from several bell curves mixed together: p(x) = sum over k of pi_k x N(x | mean_k, covariance_k), where the mixing weights pi_k add up to 1. It does soft clustering: each point gets a probability of belonging to every cluster, called its responsibility. The Expectation-Maximisation (EM) algorithm alternates two steps. E-step: compute responsibilities r_k = pi_k x N_k(x) / sum of pi_j x N_j(x). M-step: update each mean, covariance and weight using those responsibilities. Worked example: weights 0.5 and 0.5 with likelihoods 0.2 and 0.05 at a point give 0.1 and 0.025, so r_1 = 0.1 / 0.125 = 0.8." },
  { id: "clu-5", category: "Clustering", topic: "K-means vs GMM", content: "K-means gives each point to exactly one cluster (hard assignment) and implicitly assumes round clusters of similar size. A GMM gives each point a probability for every cluster (soft assignment) and learns a separate covariance per cluster, so it can fit stretched, tilted, ellipse-shaped clusters of different sizes. K-means behaves like a GMM with equal round clusters and hard assignments. GMM is slower, can get stuck in local optima like k-means, and can be unstable when a cluster has too few points. Use case: a customer who sits between two groups gets a 60/40 split from a GMM instead of being forced into one." },
  { id: "clu-6", category: "Clustering", topic: "DBSCAN", content: "DBSCAN groups points by density instead of distance to a centre. It has two settings: eps (how close counts as a neighbour) and minPts (how many neighbours make a dense area). A point with at least minPts neighbours within eps is a core point, clusters grow by linking core points, and points in no dense area are labelled noise. Unlike k-means it does not need k, can find curved or odd-shaped clusters, and flags outliers. It struggles when different clusters have very different densities. Use case: finding crowded areas of shops on a map while ignoring lonely stalls." },
  { id: "clu-7", category: "Sequence Models", topic: "Hidden Markov Models", content: "A Hidden Markov Model (HMM) describes a sequence where a hidden state changes over time and each state produces something we can observe. It has three parts: initial probabilities pi (where we start), a transition matrix A (chance of moving from one state to another) and an emission matrix B (chance a state produces each observation). The Markov assumption says the next state depends only on the current state, not the whole history. Classic example: the hidden weather (rainy or sunny) and the observed activity (walk, shop, clean). Use cases: older speech recognition, part-of-speech tagging and gene finding." },
  { id: "clu-8", category: "Sequence Models", topic: "HMM Probability Calculations", calc: true, content: "Worked example. Hidden states Rainy (R) and Sunny (S). Start: P(R) = 0.6, P(S) = 0.4. Transitions: R to R 0.7, R to S 0.3, S to R 0.4, S to S 0.6. Emissions: P(walk | R) = 0.1, P(shop | S) = 0.4. Probability of the state sequence R, S, S = 0.6 x 0.3 x 0.6 = 0.108. Joint probability of states R, S and observations walk, shop = P(R) x P(walk|R) x P(S|R) x P(shop|S) = 0.6 x 0.1 x 0.3 x 0.4 = 0.0072. Rule: multiply the start probability, then for each step multiply the transition and the emission. Each row of a transition matrix adds up to 1." },
  { id: "clu-9", category: "Sequence Models", topic: "Forward Algorithm, Viterbi and Baum-Welch", content: "An HMM solves three classic problems. Evaluation: how likely is this observation sequence? The forward algorithm answers it by summing over all possible state paths with dynamic programming. Decoding: what is the single most likely hidden state sequence? The Viterbi algorithm answers it by keeping the maximum instead of the sum at each step. Learning: how do we estimate the probabilities from data? The Baum-Welch algorithm does it, and it is EM applied to HMMs. Dynamic programming makes both forward and Viterbi cost about N^2 x T steps for N states and T time steps, instead of N^T for checking every path." },

  // --- Tokenization, embeddings, positional encoding, attention math ---
  { id: "nlp-1", category: "NLP", topic: "Tokenization: Word, Character and Subword", content: "Tokenization splits text into pieces (tokens) that a model turns into numbers. Word-level tokens are easy to read but the vocabulary becomes huge and unseen words become unknown tokens. Character-level tokens never meet an unknown word but make sequences very long. Subword tokenization (BPE, WordPiece, SentencePiece) keeps common words whole and splits rare words into known pieces, such as 'unhappiness' into 'un', 'happi', 'ness'. It balances vocabulary size against sequence length and copes with misspellings and names. Use case: speech transcripts in mixed languages like Pidgin and Kinyarwanda-English still get split into known pieces." },
  { id: "nlp-2", category: "NLP", topic: "Byte Pair Encoding (BPE) Merges", calc: true, content: "BPE builds a subword vocabulary by repeatedly merging the most frequent adjacent pair of tokens into a new token. Worked example: start with the characters of 'aaabdaaabac'. The adjacent pairs are aa, aa, ab, bd, da, aa, aa, ab, ba, ac, so 'aa' appears 4 times and 'ab' 2 times. Merge aa into a new token Z: the text becomes 'ZabdZabac'. Next the most frequent pair is 'Za' (2 times) and it is merged too. Each merge adds one token to the vocabulary, so the number of merges decides the final vocabulary size. A bigger vocabulary means shorter sequences but a larger embedding table." },
  { id: "nlp-3", category: "NLP", topic: "Token IDs and the Embedding Matrix", calc: true, content: "Each token gets an integer ID. The embedding matrix has one row per token, so its shape is vocab_size x embedding_dim, and looking up a token ID just picks that row (the same as multiplying a one-hot vector by the matrix). Parameters = vocab_size x embedding_dim. Worked example: a vocabulary of 50,000 tokens with 768-number embeddings has 50,000 x 768 = 38,400,000 parameters. A sentence of 10 tokens becomes a 10 x 768 matrix. The rows start random and are learned during training so that tokens used in similar ways end up with similar vectors." },
  { id: "nlp-4", category: "NLP", topic: "Word Embeddings: Word2Vec Idea", content: "A word embedding is a list of numbers that places a word in space so that similar words sit close together. Word2Vec learns them from the idea that words used in similar contexts have similar meanings. Skip-gram predicts the surrounding words from one word, and CBOW predicts a word from its surrounding words. Famous result: vector('king') - vector('man') + vector('woman') lands near vector('queen'). Word2Vec gives one fixed vector per word, so 'bank' (river) and 'bank' (money) share a vector. Contextual models like BERT give a different vector for each use of the word. Use case: search that finds 'cheap phone' when the user types 'affordable mobile'." },
  { id: "nlp-5", category: "NLP", topic: "Comparing Embeddings with Cosine Similarity", calc: true, content: "Closeness of two word vectors is measured with cosine similarity: cos = (a . b) / (|a| x |b|). Worked example with tiny 2-number embeddings: cat = (0.9, 0.1), dog = (0.8, 0.2), car = (0.1, 0.9). cat . dog = 0.72 + 0.02 = 0.74 and |cat| x |dog| = 0.906 x 0.825 = 0.747, so cos(cat, dog) = 0.99. cat . car = 0.09 + 0.09 = 0.18 and |cat| x |car| = 0.82, so cos(cat, car) = 0.22. Cat is much closer to dog than to car. Cosine ignores vector length, so a long and a short vector pointing the same way are still a perfect match." },
  { id: "nlp-6", category: "NLP", topic: "Sinusoidal Positional Encoding", calc: true, content: "Self-attention looks at all tokens at once and has no sense of order, so we add a position signal to each embedding. Sinusoidal positional encoding uses PE(pos, 2i) = sin(pos / 10000^(2i/d)) and PE(pos, 2i+1) = cos(pos / 10000^(2i/d)), where pos is the token position and d the embedding size. Worked example with d = 4: at pos = 0 every sin is 0 and every cos is 1, giving (0, 1, 0, 1). At pos = 1 the first pair is (sin 1, cos 1) = (0.84, 0.54) and the second pair uses 10000^(2/4) = 100, giving (sin 0.01, cos 0.01) = (0.01, 1.00). Early dimensions change fast and later ones change slowly, like the hands of a clock, so every position gets a unique pattern. The encoding is added to the token embedding and has no learned parameters." },
  { id: "nlp-7", category: "NLP", topic: "Learned, Sinusoidal and Rotary Position Methods", content: "Learned positional embeddings are a trainable table with one vector per position, simple and effective but limited to the maximum length seen in training. Sinusoidal encodings use a fixed formula, need no parameters, and can be computed for any position. Rotary Position Embedding (RoPE) rotates the query and key vectors by an angle that grows with position, so the attention score depends on the distance between two tokens rather than their absolute positions, and it is widely used in modern large language models. All three solve the same problem: attention alone treats 'dog bites man' and 'man bites dog' as the same bag of words." },
  { id: "nlp-8", category: "NLP", topic: "Scaled Dot-Product Attention Math", calc: true, content: "Attention(Q, K, V) = softmax(Q K^T / sqrt(d_k)) V. Each token's query is compared (dot product) with every key to get scores, softmax turns the scores into weights that add up to 1, and the output is the weighted mix of the value vectors. Dividing by sqrt(d_k) matters because dot products grow with the vector size (their variance is about d_k), and big scores make softmax too spiky, which kills gradients. With d_k = 64 we divide by 8. Worked example: scores 2 and 0 give softmax weights e^2/(e^2+1) = 0.88 and 0.12, so the token mostly copies the first value vector." },
  { id: "nlp-9", category: "NLP", topic: "TF-IDF Calculation", calc: true, content: "TF-IDF scores how important a word is to one document compared with a whole collection: tf-idf = tf x log10(N / df), where tf is how often the word appears in the document, N is the number of documents and df is the number of documents containing the word. Worked example: a word appears 3 times in a document and in 10 of 100 documents, so idf = log10(100/10) = 1 and tf-idf = 3 x 1 = 3. A word found in every document has idf = log10(1) = 0, so its score is 0 however often it appears (like 'the'). Use case: picking the keywords that best describe a transcript." },
  { id: "nlp-10", category: "NLP", topic: "Perplexity of a Language Model", calc: true, content: "Perplexity measures how surprised a language model is by real text: perplexity = exp(average negative log-probability per token). Lower is better. A simple way to read it: perplexity N means the model is as unsure as if it were choosing between N equally likely tokens at every step. Worked example: a model that gives every correct token probability 0.25 has perplexity 1 / 0.25 = 4. If it gives every correct token probability 0.5, perplexity is 2. A model that guesses uniformly over a 50,000-token vocabulary has perplexity 50,000. Use case: comparing two models on the same text before choosing one." },
  { id: "cap-21", category: "Speech ML", topic: "Computing Word Error Rate by Hand", calc: true, content: "WER = (substitutions + deletions + insertions) / number of words in the reference. Worked example: reference 'the cat sat on the mat' has 6 words. The model heard 'the cat sit on mat'. 'sat' became 'sit' (1 substitution) and the second 'the' is missing (1 deletion), so errors = 2 and WER = 2/6 = 0.33, or 33%. WER can go above 100% if the model adds many extra words, which is exactly what ASR hallucination in silence does. Use case: comparing two speech models on the same noisy shop recordings." },
];

